{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>But you has HaShem taken, etc., to be unto Him for a people of inheritance, as on this day.</b> Above he said, ״וּפֶן תִּשָּׂא עֵינֶיךָ הַשָּׁמַיְמָה וְרָאִיתָ אֶת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״, etc., and that is the great daily sphere, the greatest of all the host of heaven. Concerning this he said: how could you be so bereft of knowledge as to bow down to the sun, when you are considered as great and elevated before HaShem, blessed be He, as the sun itself — so how could you serve that which is like you, as it says (Judges 5:31), ״וְאֹהֲבָיו כְּצֵאת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ בִּגְבוּרָתוֹ״? And if you are comparable in rank to the sun, then all the more so to the rest of the host of heaven, which are lesser in order than the sun. And indeed, you exercise dominion over the sun, as in ״שֶׁמֶשׁ בְּגִבְעוֹן דּוֹם״ (Joshua 10:12), and likewise Moses halted the sun. And as for what is written, ״אֲשֶׁר חָלַק ה׳ לְכָל הָעַמִּים״, he did not say this concerning the existence of their light, but concerning the fact that their governance comes through intermediaries, for all the nations are delivered into the hand of the celestial array, whereas you HaShem took for His direct governance, for you are governed by the First Cause, blessed be He. Therefore you have dominion over the sun — so how could you serve it?" "Another matter: <b>״as on this day״ refers to what is said, ״But you HaShem took from the iron furnace.״</b> The refiner who purifies silver from all dross and impurity until it becomes clean and pure and bright as the sun — so your material essence was refined through the affliction of Egypt until it became clear as the sun. Concerning this he said, ״to be unto Him for a people of inheritance as on this day״ — like the daily sphere, as it is written: ״וְאֹהֲבָיו כְּצֵאת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ בִּגְבוּרָתוֹ״. And likewise ״מִכְרָה כַיּוֹם״ (Genesis 25:31), whose Targum is ״כְּיוֹם דְּלָהֵן״ — which is clear without dross or impurity; so too, ״sell me a sale clear and bright as the sun.״ And some say ״as on this day״ means like the sun, which does not labor for the sake of receiving reward. And it appears to me that it is concerning this that it is said ״וְאֹהֲבָיו כְּצֵאת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ — these are those who serve out of love." "And as for what is written, <b>״וַה׳ הִתְאַנַּף בִּי עַל דִּבְרֵיכֶם״,</b> the commentators were troubled by this verse, for it has no connection neither to what precedes nor to what follows, and they offered forced interpretations. And it is plausible to hear it as coming to resolve: if the sun causes creatures to sin, why is it not destroyed, in the manner that Moses was punished for causing the multitudes to sin? Concerning this he said that the case does not parallel the proof — for it is true that HaShem was angry with me on account of your words, but my death does not cause destruction to the multitudes, for though I die I do not cross the Jordan; in any event, you remain standing and cross the Jordan. But the sun — if He destroyed it, then it would destroy the entire world. And would He destroy His world because of fools who corrupted themselves? As is concluded in tractate Avodah Zarah (54b), a philosopher asked the elders in Rome: if the Holy One, blessed be He, does not desire idolatry, why does He not destroy it, etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:27.790266767Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}